\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2.1}{\ignorespaces Examples of functional and dysfunctional wastewater treatment components. The straight structures crossing the aerators are walkways that carry the air pipes feeding the diffusers. The bar across each clarifier is a rotating scraper bridge.}}{4}{figure.caption.9}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.1}{\ignorespaces Number of labelled images per month for each of the five facilities. Cape Flats and Waterval have a separate image for each unit on most dates, so their cells can be larger than one.}}{17}{figure.caption.15}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.2}{\ignorespaces Location of the five WWTW facilities in the dataset.}}{18}{figure.caption.16}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.3}{\ignorespaces Overview of the Cape Flats WWTW facility. The facility consists of four reactor units (CF1--CF4) placed side by side. Each unit has rectangular aerobic zones at the top and four circular clarifiers below them.}}{18}{figure.caption.17}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.4}{\ignorespaces One example of each aerobic zone class, and the facility to which it belongs. Each example is a test image from the chronological split (Section~\ref {sec:exp_mixed}) that all four pretrained models classified correctly with the highest mean confidence, so that it is a clear case of its class.}}{19}{figure.caption.18}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.5}{\ignorespaces One example of each clarifier class, and the facility to which it belongs, chosen in the same way as in Figure~\ref {fig:aerobic_examples}. The area outside each tank is masked to black by the preparation script (Section~\ref {sec:pipeline}).}}{19}{figure.caption.19}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.6}{\ignorespaces A relabelled clarifier from Cape Flats, shown with some of its surroundings. The original label was ``Scum'', but the label was corrected to ``Empty'', since the tank appears to be drained, with its floor and the shadow of the bridge visible inside it.}}{22}{figure.caption.21}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.1}{\ignorespaces Experimental procedure, from labelled satellite images to one results file per component, architecture and data split.}}{24}{figure.caption.24}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.2}{\ignorespaces Augmentations applied to a single \textbf {Scum} clarifier from Cape Flats. Top row: the original image, followed by a horizontal flip, a rotation of $15^\circ $, and a random colour jitter, each applied on its own. Bottom row: four random outputs of the full augmentation pipeline (flip, rotation and colour jitter combined).}}{26}{figure.caption.26}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.3}{\ignorespaces The training process used for every architecture: an epoch of training and development-set evaluation, a scheduler step, a checkpoint decision based on development loss, and a stopping check repeated every epoch until training ends.}}{30}{figure.caption.30}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6.1}{\ignorespaces Confusion matrices of the architecture with the highest mean AUC in Experiment~2 for each component, on the held-out facility used for the Grad-CAM analysis (Section~\ref {sec:results_gradcam}). The confusion matrices of the other held-out facilities are given in Appendix~\ref {appen:res_lofo}. Each row is normalised by the number of test images of its true class (n), so a cell gives the fraction of that class assigned to the predicted class, with the count in brackets.}}{38}{figure.caption.35}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6.2}{\ignorespaces Grad-CAM heatmaps of the ConvNeXt-Tiny clarifier model, for test images from Fisantekraal, which was held out during training. Top: one correctly classified clarifier per class. Bottom: one clarifier from each of the four most common mistakes. Every heatmap is calculated for the assigned class, and $p$ is the softmax probability of that class. Red regions contributed the most to the classification.}}{41}{figure.caption.39}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6.3}{\ignorespaces Grad-CAM heatmaps of the DenseNet-121 aerobic zone model, for test images from Waterval, which was held out during training. Top: one correctly classified aerobic zone per class. Bottom: one aerobic zone from each of the three most common mistakes. Every heatmap is calculated for the assigned class, and $p$ is the softmax probability of that class. Red regions contributed the most to the classification.}}{42}{figure.caption.40}%
